# How to Only Use created_at in Laravel

Laravel's `Model` class automatically manages two columns by default: `created_at` and `updated_at`. This behavior is controlled by the `$timestamps` property and the `CREATED_AT` and `UPDATED_AT` constants. While disabling timestamps entirely is a common pattern for read-only models or specific legacy integrations, developers often encounter a scenario where they need to retain automatic creation tracking while removing the automatic update logic.

Laravel does not provide a built-in configuration flag to simply "turn off" the updater while keeping the creator intact. The framework treats timestamp management as a binary state: either both columns are managed automatically, or neither is. To achieve your specific requirementâusing `created_at` exclusivelyâyou must manually intervene in how the model interacts with the database and its own state.

The most direct approach involves modifying the model's behavior to prevent Laravel from touching the `updated_at` column during mass assignments or direct property assignment. This can be achieved by overriding the `$fillable` property if you are using mass assignment guards, but a more robust solution for automatic updates is to customize the `$touches` array or override specific mutators/accessors. However, since Laravel 8 and later, the behavior is tightly coupled to the `$timestamps` flag.

If your goal is to keep `created_at` auto-generated but stop `updated_at` from updating automatically when you save a model, the standard `public $timestamps = true;` setting will not suffice on its own. You must explicitly tell Laravel not to touch the update column. This is typically done by defining a custom accessor or mutator for the `updated_at` column that simply returns the existing value without modification, or by ensuring your save logic does not trigger the internal `$touch` method for that specific field.

A cleaner architectural pattern is to define your model such that it only fills the `created_at` column during mass assignment and ignores `updated_at`. You can do this by setting `$fillable` to include only `created_at` (and other necessary fields like `name`, `email`, etc.) and explicitly excluding `updated_at`. When you perform a `$model->save()` operation, Laravel will generate the timestamp for `created_at` if it is null, but because `updated_at` is not in the fillable array, any attempt to mass-assign it will be ignored. Crucially, this does not automatically update the column if you are assigning other properties directly; however, Laravel's internal logic often still attempts to update the timestamp unless explicitly prevented.

To strictly prevent the automatic update of `updated_at` while preserving `created_at`, you should override the `save` method or use a custom trait that disables the `$touch` behavior for the update column. Here is an implementation example using a model trait:

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

trait TimestampOnlyCreated
{
    public function save(array $options = [])
    {
        // Prevent automatic touching of updated_at
        $this->setAppends([]); 

        return parent::save($options);
    }
}
```

While the above trait approach is a starting point, a more precise method without overriding core `save` logic is to define the model's `$touches` property. Laravel allows you to specify which columns should be automatically updated via the `$touches` array. However, standard Eloquent models do not expose this publicly by default in the same way they do for `$fillable`.

The most reliable solution that adheres to strict requirements without complex overrides is to manage timestamps manually within the `boot()` method or rely on the fact that if you do not assign the `updated_at` attribute and do not set `$timestamps = false`, Laravel will only update it if your model explicitly marks it for update.

Consider this configuration:

```php
class Product extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'price', 'created_at'];

    // Disable automatic updating of updated_at by ensuring it's not in fillable
    // and never assigning to it directly in mass operations.

    public function setUpdatedAt($value)
    {
        // Override setter to do nothing, preventing auto-updates
        return $this;
    }
}
```

By overriding the `setUpdatedAt` method to be a no-op (or throwing an exception if you want to enforce it strictly), you effectively break the automatic update cycle. Laravel checks for this mutator before writing to the database. If the setter does nothing, the column remains untouched unless explicitly set in your controller or request payload.

This approach ensures that `created_at` is still generated automatically by Eloquent (as long as the value is null at the time of insertion), while `updated_at` remains static after the initial save, provided you do not manually assign to it. This gives you granular control over data integrity without disabling the entire timestamp system.

If you require a more sophisticated solution or need to handle this across many models, consider creating a dedicated trait that handles the `$touches` logic or customizes the model bootstrapping process. For deeper dives into Eloquent internals and advanced timestamp management patterns, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide further context on maintaining data consistency in high-volume applications.
